 the  ibright cl 1500 imaging system  and we compare it to x-ray you can  really see an improvement in sensitivity  uh by looking at this data that we show  here so we we we're looking at plate  data um and we're exposing at 10 30 60  120 and 300 seconds  now we can see down to one two three  four five maybe six  wells um on that 10 second exposure with  the eyebrite compared to only three on  the x-ray film  so you really get a lot better  sensitivity with a digital imager  particularly with an eyebrite imager and  when you're looking at low abundant  proteins that can make all the  difference  these graphs really kind of just take  the previous slide and put it in  graphical form  but what this does is it highlights not  the sensitivity but the dynamic range  that you  get out of that eyebrite  even the x-ray film for the wells that  it detected you can see the the dynamic  range is  relatively very small compared to the  large dynamic range of the eyebrite  imager this is going to allow you to get  better sensitivity but you're also going  to be able to distinguish  expression amounts better  if you have one cell  that maybe you treated with some drug  and you're trying to see how much  protein is being expressed compared to a  control  you may not be able to see a difference  with film but you can pick up on that  difference from the eyebright imager  because it has a wider dynamic range and  you can more easily distinguish between  expression levels  in that point  is really driven home on this next slide  where you can get better signal  linearity you can see down here um  if you look where it says where we have  the letter d there  um  that's x-ray film and you can see it  saturates out that that linear signal  hits a plateau once you hit that plateau  you cannot distinguish between different  uh  expression levels or lysate loads  because you're getting the same signal  between the two different expression  levels if you have a linear  signal  that can that is made possible by that  wide dynamic range  then you can really get not only can you  distinguish between your expression  levels you can even quantitate your  western blot results  okay so i'm just going to  wrap up with kind of a summary on what  we just talked about in terms of  detecting low abundance proteins so the  first thing is to choose an appropriate  gel chemistry for your protein of  interest we recommend the biz tris gel  chemistry because it has less protein  degradation  you can load more sample using wedgewell  format gels  use a more sensitive chemiluminescent  detection reagent we suggest using super  signal west ato  test numerous fluorescent secondary  antibodies when when performing  fluorescence  consider a signal enhancer uh to boost  the signal and limit the amount of  background and really optimize that  signal to noise ratio and then utilize  digital imaging for improved signal  sensitivity and getting that wide  dynamic range  and that's all i have for part two of  our webinar today i really appreciate  your attention  um  and  i look forward to answering questions  before we get there  i do want to mention that we do have a  part three of this webinar that will be  presented by kelly feather hennigan  on october 14th at 9 00 a.m pacific time  12 p.m eastern time  5 p.m gmt and 3 a.m aet  finally  don't forget to check out our new  updated handbooks these are your go-to  guides for gel electrophoresis and  western blotting  basically what these are is they're a  comprehensive easy to use guide that  covers every procedural step from sample  preparation all the way to imaging and  data analysis and you can find other  educational resources and links  including how-to videos webinars and  selection to tools on our website at  thermofisher.com  western handbook you can also use the qr  code that is at the bottom of your  screen to navigate there  okay now i'm ready to take some  questions  thank you caleb for your informative  presentation  we will now start the live q a portion  of the webinar if you have a question  you would like to ask please do so now  just click on the ask a question box  located on the far left of your screen  we'll answer as many of your questions  as we have time for  let's get started our first question is  so should five percent milk also be  avoided as a blocking buffer for  fluorescence detection  jennifer sorry i was  working on getting unmuted can you  repeat the question  certainly  should five percent milk also be avoided  as a blocking buffer for fluorescence  detection  so  the data that we showed today um as part  of this presentation showed that while  five percent milk can work in in a  fluorescence application  we recommend our blocker fl because  we've seen that you can actually get  better sensitivity and better signal to  noise ratio  all right thank you caleb the next  question here  i use the start blocking buffer to block  my membrane can i reuse it if i can how  many times can i use it how long will it  be good and do you recommend to dilute  antibodies using the start blocking  buffer or use 5 bsa  so you can to answer the second part of  that question first you can certainly  use  starting block blocking buffer to dilute  your primary antibody  i would not recommend storing your  primary antibody in starting block  i think you can store your primary  antibody either  in  in the buffer that it came in or five  percent bsa  with regard to  reusing starting block blocking buffer  i would not recommend it  when you when you reuse a blocking  buffer  you're changing the formulation a little  bit because a lot of that protein is  binding to the membrane  and so you're losing a little bit of  of protein in your blocking buffer what  that can do is it can impact your  reproducibility because you're not using  the exact same blocking buffer  on your next experiment that you used on  the previous experiment so i would  recommend using fresh blocking buffer  each time you block your block  instead of reusing it  thank you again caleb our next question  here  for fluorescence you make the antibiotic  do you make the antibodies in the  blocker fl buffer 2  uh yeah so  similar to the previous question you can  certainly dilute your primary antibodies  in the blocking buffer  but again i would not recommend storing  your primary antibodies in the blocker  sl blocking buffer  all right great thank you again  our next question  in choosing the blocking buffer what  would be the difference between the tbs  or pbs based blocking buffer will it  make a difference if i switch between  the two  and what should be considered in picking  the blocking solution  one of the  the biggest considerations when choosing  tbs versus pbs is what enzyme you're  using for detection  so if you're using an hrp conjugated  secondary antibody  compared to an alkaline phosphatase  conjugated secondary antibody the choice  becomes very important  pbs because it's a phosphate buffered  saline will interfere with an alkaline  phosphatase enzyme and so pbs should be  avoided  when when using an alkaline phosphatase  enzyme  if you're using an hrp conjugated enzyme  in most cases  um  either buffer should should be able to  work for your system  unless you  unless primary amines  impact your your system i i think that's  relatively uncommon  uh to have to accommodate  the primary amine that's present in your  tris buffer  and so if you're kind of going with a  normal very common western bottom setup  and you're using an hrp conjugated  secondary antibody then you should be  able to use either tbs or pbs  all right thank you caleb our next  question what type of blocking buffer  would you recommend for a goat antibody  we tried milk and bfa at different  concentrations and different blocking  times and got high background and low  signal  so i would recommend our starting block  blocking buffer for this application it  does perform well with with goat  secondary antibodies  it sounds like this this is uh one of  those situations where you may have a  delicate or a  a  a secondary antibody that  doesn't like to play well with other  reagents  so i would recommend using our starting  block blocking buffer  but there may be some additional  optimization that you'll have to do  all right thank you caleb our next  question here if a routine ecl substrate  is used first and no bands are seen can  i use the high sensitivity substrates  directly after without stripping and  re-probing  yes  you do not need to strip and read probe  to use a higher sensitivity ecl  substrate you should be able to  maybe do a quick wash a quick rinse on  your blot and then add the  higher sensitivity ecl substrate  thank you caleb  okay next question here why do you  recommend using higher antibody  dilutions when using high sensitivity  substrates  i just want to make a quick  clarification on this question when  we're talking about higher antibody  dilutions  we're talking about lower antibody  concentration  so  right so that means that you're diluting  your antibody more which means you have  less antibody that you're applying to  the western blot  and the reason why we recommend  going with higher antibody dilutions at  least to start off with is we have seen  that some of these ecl substrates if  you're using a very high sensitivity ecl  substrate such as super signal west ato  it can it can blow out your block  because it is so powerful  uh you'll get so much signal that you  won't be able to distinguish  two adjacent bands and so backing off on  that on that antibody concentration is  one way to ensure good results the first  time  now  if you back off on your antibody  concentration and you're not seeing  signal then you can uh optimize your  block by increasing that antibody  concentration again  but you should never go from a low  performing or an entry level ecl  substrate straight to the high  sensitivity substrates  because you will see a lot of signal  because they are very powerful  substrates  all right great thank you caleb got a  couple more questions here  i notice you did not mention super  signal west femto when you talked about  high sensitivity substrates will you be  discontinuing that product  no if you are a super signal west fento  user um  you'll be happy to hear that we do not  plan to discontinue that product  we have introduced super signal west ato  which does provide  better sensitivity  so you can get even more detection of  low abundant proteins however super  signal west femto is also a very  sensitive substrate so if that is  working for your system feel free to  continue using it we do not plan to  discontinue  great thank you caleb our last question  we've got time for one more  does the choice of membrane influence  background and chemiluminescence  detection  um  so the the choice of membrane does not  have as much of an impact in  chemiluminescent detection as as it does  in fluorescence detection  um fluorescence detection as we showed  previously  you can get a lot of background  depending on the  the  wavelength that you are detecting  but we don't see the same type of  contribution to background from a  specific type of membrane  in chemiluminescent detection  thank you caleb  do you have any final comments for our  audience  um just that i really appreciate your  attendance um i'm happy to  have presented and answered your  questions a very engaged audience so  thank you for your attention  and  have a good rest your day good luck with  your western bots  thank you again caleb for your time  today we would also like to thank lab  roots and our sponsor thermo fisher  scientific for underwriting today's  educational webcast  before we go i'd like to thank the  audience for joining us today and for  their interesting questions  questions we did not have time for today  and those submitted during the on-demand  period will be addressed by the speaker  via the contact information you provided  at the time of registration  this webcast can be viewed on demand lab  roots will alert you via email when it's  available for replay we encourage you to  share that email with your colleagues  who may have missed today's live event  until next time  goodbye
 

